In Java, the `substring()` method allows you to extract a portion of a string starting from a specified index. This method is useful when you need to retrieve part of a string from a specific position until the end of the original string.

**Syntax:**
```java
string.substring(startIndex)
```

- **startIndex**: The position from where the substring begins (inclusive).
- **Return Type**: A new string containing characters from `startIndex` to the end of the original string.

---

**Example:**
```java
class Seedit {
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 // Initialize a string with a sample text
 String text = "Hello, World!";

 // Extract a substring starting from index 7 ("World!")
 String result = text.substring(7);

 // Print the extracted substring
 System.out.println("Extracted Substring: " + result);
 }
}
```

**Expected Output:**
```default
Extracted Substring: World!
```

